<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Area (Section 6.1)</title>

  <exercise workspace="2.4in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given the two parabolas <m>y=x^2-5</m> and <m>y=3-x^2</m>,
        sketch their graphs and find the area of the region between
        them.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="06">
      <image width="50%">
        <shortdescription>
          A graph of two parabolas, one opening up and one opening down
        </shortdescription>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                   label="06-area-1">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/06-area-1.xml"/>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            A\amp=\int_{-2}{2} [(3-x^2)-(x^2-5)]~dx
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp=\int_{-2}{2} (8-2x^2)~dx
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp=(8x-x^2)|_{-2}^2
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp=64/3
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2.4in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Graph the functions <m>f(x) = \cos x</m> and
        <m>g(x)=\sin x</m> and find the area of one of the regions
        bounded by their graphs.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="06">
      <image width="50%">
        <shortdescription>
          A graph of sine x and cosine x
        </shortdescription>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                   label="06-area-2">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/06-area-2.xml"/>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            A\amp=\int_{\pi/4}^{\pi/4} (\sin(x)-\cos(x)~dx
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp=(-\cos(x)-\sin(x))|_{\pi/4}^{3\pi/4}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp=\sqrt{2}
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the area between the graphs of <m>y = x^3</m> and
        <m>y=x</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="06">
      <image width="50%">
        <shortdescription>
          A graph of x cubed and a graph of x
        </shortdescription>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                   label="06-area-3">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/06-area-3.xml"/>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            A\amp=\int_{-1}^{0} (x^3-x)~dx+\int_{0}^{1} (x-x^3)~dx
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp=(\frac14x^4-\frac12x^2)|_{-1}^0 +
            (\frac12x^2-\frac14x^4)|_0^1
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp=1/2
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The curve <m>x=y^2-12</m> and the line <m>y=x</m> are shown
        in the figure below.  I would like for you to find the region
        between them by slicing the region into <em>horizontal</em>
        rectangles as shown.  What is the width of each rectangle?
        What is the height?  How do you then find the area?
      </p>
      <image width="40%">
        <shortdescription>
          Two curves where x is defined in terms of y
        </shortdescription>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                   label="06-area-4">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/06-area-4.xml"/>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </statement>
    <solution component="06">
      <p>
        The width of the rectangle is given by the difference in the
        <m>x</m> coordinates so it is <m>y-(y^2-12)</m> and the height
        is a small change in <m>y</m> so <m>dy</m>.  This means that
        the area of the rectangle is <m>(y-y^2+12)~dy</m>.  The curves
        intersect when their <m>x</m> coordinates are equal, which is
        when
        <md>
          <mrow>
            y \amp = y^2-12
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            y^2-y-12 \amp = 0
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            (y-4)(y+3) \amp = 0
          </mrow>
        </md>
        so <m>y=-3</m> and <m>y=4</m>.  This means that the area is
        <md>
          A=\int_{-3}^{4} (y-y^2+12)~dy = 343/6 = 51.17
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="3in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose you need to paint the region between the graph
        <m>y=\sqrt{x}</m> and the <m>x</m>-axis.  You start painting
        at <m>x=0</m> and work your way to the right.  However, you
        only have enough paint to cover 18 square units of area.
        Where does your paint run out?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="06">
      <image width="50%">
        <shortdescription>
          Two graph of y equals square root of x
        </shortdescription>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                   label="06-area-5">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/06-area-5.xml"/>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
      <p>
        Call <m>R</m> the right endpoint where the paint runs out.  We
        have
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_0^R \sqrt{x}\amp=18
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \frac23 R^{3/2}\amp =18
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            R\amp = 9
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Sometimes we can use area to help us evaluate a definite
        integral.  For instance, the circle of radius 5 centered at
        the origin is described by <m>x^2+y^2 = 25</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write the
          upper-half of this circle by solving for <m>y</m> in terms
          of <m>x</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="06">
        <p>
          <m>y=\sqrt{25-x^2}</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What does this tell you about the integral
          <m>\ds\int_{-5}^5 \sqrt{25-x^2}~dx</m> or the integral
          <m>\ds\int_{0}^5 \sqrt{25-x^2}~dx</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="06">
        <p>
          The first is half the area of the circle so
          <md>
            \int_{-5}^5 \sqrt{25-x^2}~dx = \frac12\pi5^2
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Apply the same kind of thinking to evaluate the integral
          <m>\ds\int_{-2}^4 |x|~dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="06">
        <p>
          Drawing a graph, this is the area of two triangles so
          <md>
            \int_{-2}^4 |x|~dx = 2 + 8 = 10
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Sometimes we want to be able to work with situations having an
        unknown parameter in them.  For instance, if <m>a</m> is some
        unknown constant, find the area between the graph
        <m>y=a-x^2</m> and the <m>x</m>-axis.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="06">
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            A \amp = \int_{-\sqrt{a}}^{\sqrt{a}} (a-x^2)~dx
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            A \amp = (ax - \frac13 x^3)|_{-\sqrt{a}}^{\sqrt{a}}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            A \amp = \frac 43 a^{3/2}
          </mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
